  "query_preview": ".. (Thursday Afternoon through Mid-Next Week)\n\nThe upper-level trough over the southwest U.S. is projected to \nopen and eject northeastward across the Mid-Mississippi Valley \nby midday Thursday, aligning parallel to a slow-moving, surface \nfrontal boundary. The coincidence of strong upper dynamics, a \nrich tropical moisture plume, and favorable frontal forcing will\ntrigger multiple rounds of widespread rainfall from Thursday \nafternoon through midday Friday. \n\nLatest REFS probabilistic guidance places 10th-to-90th \npercentile QPF totals between 0.25\" and 1.25\" across most areas\neast of the Illinois River Valley into southeast Illinois. \nHowever, high moisture flux and frontogenetical forcing along \nthe slow-moving boundary favor the development of narrow, \nmesoscale rain bands. Where these bands develop, localized \ntotals of 2\" to 3\" remain well within the realm of possibility \nacross the CWA.\n\nConditions will dry out rapidly from northwest to southeast \nFriday evening as the frontal zone clears the area and an \nexpansive Continental Polar (cP) high pressure system builds \neastward across the Midwest and Great Lakes. Cold air advection \nin the wake of the front will usher in a distinct airmass \ntransformation, replacing early-week unseasonable warmth with a \nclassic autumn pattern. High temperatures Saturday through early\nnext week will drop into the upper 60s, with overnight lows \ndipping into the 40s. Ensemble guidance maintains high \nconfidence that this broad Canadian high will remain anchored \nover the region through at least midweek, yielding an extended \nstretch of quiet, crisp fall weather.",
